{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791982708654,"sessionID":"ses_ec5831f61ffe1i8pfHMAPn6Hsv","part":{"partID":"prt_13a7d079d0011CJJnSIQLJbDNq","sessionID":"ses_ec5831f61ffe1i8pfHMAPn6Hsv","messageID":"msg_13a7d031d00131aCJyVJqXIWiB","type":"tool","id":"toolu_vrtx_012tAcCzEemK5raDzA4Y58NL","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"artifacts/result.md","content":"# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Source statements evaluated:**\n\n- **R1:** \"When a request is complete, it shall process it appropriately.\"\n- **R2:** \"While no authenticated user exists, the user shall see their dashboard.\"\n\n**Requested outcome:** Review and evaluate two existing requirement statements as a requirements elicitor, producing a complete portable Requirements Elicitation Package.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** Unknown for both statements. Neither names a system, an actor, an interface, or a domain. Both require significant clarification before they can be implemented or verified.\n\n**Identified information kinds before drafting:**\n\n| Statement | Kind | Notes |\n|-----------|------|-------|\n| R1 | Behavioral obligation (partial) | Names no system, no actor, no defined request lifecycle, no defined completion event, no defined response |\n| R1 \"appropriately\" | Vague qualifier | Flagged per readiness gate; not a defined measure |\n| R2 | Behavioral obligation (partial) | Names no system; precondition is internally contradictory (see Consistency Findings) |\n| R2 \"no authenticated user exists\" | Precondition | Contradicts \"the user\" in the response clause |\n| R2 \"their dashboard\" | Undefined domain term | Ownership reference unclear when no user is authenticated |\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**R1 — high-value questions (any one of these blocks readiness):**\n\n1. **System boundary:** What system receives and processes the request? (e.g., an API gateway, a job scheduler, a payment processor?)\n2. **Actor and interface:** Who or what submits the request, and at which boundary does the response appear?\n3. **\"Complete\" defined:** What does it mean for a request to be complete — submitted, validated, fully executed, or acknowledged? Is \"complete\" an intermediate lifecycle state or a terminal one?\n4. **\"Process it appropriately\" defined:** What observable action must the system take? (e.g., return a confirmation, write a record, forward to another system?) \"Appropriately\" has no agreed measure; two implementers would choose different behavior.\n5. **Failure and alternate paths:** What shall the system do if the request cannot be processed, is malformed, arrives out of order, or arrives after a timeout?\n\n**R2 — high-value questions (any one of these blocks readiness):**\n\n1. **System boundary:** What system is responsible for showing the dashboard?\n2. **Intended precondition:** The phrase \"while no authenticated user exists\" combined with \"the user shall see their dashboard\" is internally contradictory. Which behavior is intended:\n   - (a) While no user is authenticated (i.e., prior to login), the system shall show a generic/public landing page?\n   - (b) While an authenticated user exists (i.e., after login), the system shall show that user's personalized dashboard?\n   - (c) Something else?\n3. **\"Their dashboard\" defined:** Whose dashboard? If no authenticated user exists, there is no \"their.\" If an authenticated user does exist, what content constitutes \"their dashboard\"?\n4. **Actor:** Who is \"the user\" — an unauthenticated visitor, an authenticated account holder, a specific role?\n5. **Interface:** At what boundary is the dashboard visible — a browser viewport, an API response, a mobile screen?\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1 (from R1)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** unresolved\n- **Requirement:** Drafting is blocked. The pattern cannot be selected without guessing. The source uses `When`, which would suggest an event-driven pattern, but \"a request is complete\" is not a defined discrete event at any named boundary. The response \"process it appropriately\" is not an observable system behavior. No system, actor, or interface is named.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R1 appears to intend that some system shall perform some defined action after a request reaches a defined completion state. The intent cannot be preserved normatively without answers to the clarifying questions above.\n\n**Implementability contract**\n- System boundary: **open** — not named in source\n- Responsible system: **open** — not named in source\n- Actor: **open** — not named in source\n- Affected interfaces: **open** — not named in source\n- Inputs and data definitions: **open** — \"a request\" is undefined; type, schema, and lifecycle state are unknown\n- Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: **open** — \"complete\" is undefined; whether this is a state or a discrete event is unresolved\n- Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: **open** — \"appropriately\" has no agreed measure; no alternate or failure response is stated\n- Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **open** — none stated\n- Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"request,\" \"complete,\" and \"appropriately\" are all undefined domain terms; pattern choice (event-driven vs. state-driven vs. complex) is unresolved\n\n**Verification contract**\n- Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: **open** — no defined request type, no defined completion event, no named system to exercise\n- Observable evidence or oracle: **open** — \"appropriately\" is not observable\n- Expected result: **open** — unknown\n- Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: **open** — none can be stated\n- Boundary, negative, and failure cases: **open** — incomplete request, malformed request, duplicate request, concurrent completion events — all unaddressed\n- Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: **open** — unknown\n- Observable at the named boundary alone: no (no boundary is named)\n- If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: cannot be determined until system boundary is defined\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2 (from R2)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** unresolved\n- **Requirement:** Drafting is blocked. The precondition \"while no authenticated user exists\" is vacuous or internally contradictory when combined with the response \"the user shall see their dashboard.\" If no authenticated user exists, there is no referent for \"the user\" or \"their dashboard.\" The EARS pattern cannot be confirmed as state-driven or event-driven without resolving the intended condition. Two interpretations produce opposite behavior:\n\n  - **Interpretation A (vacuous / unauthenticated state):** `While no authenticated session exists, the <system> shall display a <public/generic landing page> to the visitor.` — The dashboard reference is incorrect; this reads as a pre-login state.\n  - **Interpretation B (likely intended — authenticated state):** `While an authenticated user session exists, the <system> shall display that user's dashboard.` — This inverts the precondition; \"no authenticated user\" becomes \"authenticated user.\"\n\n  Neither interpretation can be confirmed without the requester's input.\n\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R2 appears to intend that a named system show a personalized view to an authenticated user, but the precondition contradicts the response. The source may contain a drafting error (negation applied incorrectly).\n\n**Implementability contract**\n- System boundary: **open** — not named in source\n- Responsible system: **open** — not named in source\n- Actor: **open** — \"the user\" is undefined when no authenticated user exists; contradictory referent\n- Affected interfaces: **open** — not named in source\n- Inputs and data definitions: **open** — \"dashboard\" content and \"authenticated user\" definition are undefined\n- Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: **open** — \"no authenticated user exists\" contradicts \"the user\"; intended precondition is unresolved\n- Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: **open** — \"see their dashboard\" is partially observable (a display appears) but content and scope are undefined; what is shown when no user is authenticated is unresolved\n- Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **open** — none stated\n- Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"authenticated user,\" \"dashboard,\" and ownership reference \"their\" are undefined; the intended precondition is materially unresolved\n\n**Verification contract**\n- Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: **open** — cannot be defined until the intended precondition is resolved; no named system to exercise\n- Observable evidence or oracle: partially observable (a UI display or API response could be inspected) but content specification is unknown\n- Expected result: **open** — unknown until precondition and dashboard definition are resolved\n- Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: **open** — none can be stated\n- Boundary, negative, and failure cases: **open** — what happens when a session expires mid-view? What if multiple authenticated users share a device? All unaddressed.\n- Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: **open** — unknown\n- Observable at the named boundary alone: no (no boundary is named)\n- If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: cannot be determined until system boundary is defined\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\nThe following suggestions are grounded in the source statements. They are not approved requirements. Each should be accepted or rejected by the requester.\n\n**Relating to R1:**\n\n1. **(Required companion)** A requirement defining what constitutes a \"complete\" request is needed before R1 can be implemented or verified. Without a defined completion state or event, the trigger in R1 is meaningless.\n\n2. **(Failure-path companion)** A requirement defining what the system shall do if a request cannot be processed (malformed, unauthorized, resource unavailable) is implied by any processing obligation and should be stated.\n\n3. **(Boundary companion)** A requirement defining the maximum time allowed between request completion and the system's response is implied if latency is a concern.\n\n**Relating to R2:**\n\n4. **(Required companion)** A requirement defining what constitutes an authenticated user session — how it is established, how it expires, and what identifies it — is needed before R2 can be implemented or verified under either interpretation.\n\n5. **(Required companion)** A requirement defining the content and scope of \"the dashboard\" is needed. What data sources, elements, or views constitute the dashboard for a given user?\n\n6. **(Failure-path companion)** A requirement defining what the system shall display when a session expires while the user is viewing the dashboard is implied by a session-scoped obligation.\n\n7. **(Failure-path companion — Interpretation A only)** If R2 intends an unauthenticated state, a companion requirement defining what the system shall display (a login prompt, a public landing page, an error) is necessary.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n### Finding 1 — Vacuous precondition in R2 (Infeasible / vacuous)\n\n**Affected statement:** R2  \n**Condition:** \"While no authenticated user exists\" combined with \"the user shall see their dashboard\"  \n**Consequence:** The pronoun \"the user\" has no referent when the precondition holds. If no authenticated user exists, there is no \"their dashboard.\" The requirement cannot be satisfied: the precondition excludes the very actor the response requires. Under standard semantics, this statement is either vacuously true (the response is never triggered because the actor never exists) or a drafting error that inverts the intended condition.  \n**Resolution options:**\n- (a) Confirm that the precondition is inverted and correct it to \"While an authenticated user session exists\" — which would make this a state-driven requirement for authenticated users.\n- (b) Confirm that the intent is an unauthenticated/pre-login state, and replace \"the user shall see their dashboard\" with a defined response for unauthenticated visitors (e.g., display a login page).\n- (c) Ask the requester to restate the intended behavior.\n\n### Finding 2 — R1 and R2 relationship unknown (Gap / Precedence question)\n\n**Affected statements:** R1, R2  \n**Condition:** R1 references \"a request\" being complete; R2 references viewing a dashboard. If the dashboard is loaded by processing a request, R1 may be intended to govern the same interaction as R2, or they may be entirely independent.  \n**Consequence:** If they are related, a precedence or sequencing rule is missing. If they are independent, no conflict exists.  \n**Resolution options:**\n- Ask whether R1 and R2 describe the same user journey or different system behaviors.\n\n### Finding 3 — Vague qualifiers block both candidates (Gap)\n\n**Affected statements:** R1 (\"appropriately\"), R2 (\"their dashboard\")  \n**Condition:** Both statements contain terms without agreed measures.  \n**Consequence:** Two reasonable implementers would produce different observable behavior for each.  \n**Resolution options:**\n- Define \"appropriately\" as one or more named observable responses.\n- Define \"their dashboard\" as a named set of content, data, or views scoped to the authenticated user's identity.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n1. **A1 (provisional):** R1 and R2 are intended to describe system obligations, not user or actor obligations. The subject \"it\" in R1 and \"the user\" in R2 are interpreted as referring to distinct parties — a system and an actor respectively — but this is unconfirmed.\n\n2. **A2 (provisional):** \"Authenticated user\" in R2 refers to a user whose identity has been verified by a login or session mechanism at the system boundary, not merely a user who has visited the system.\n\n3. **A3 (provisional):** R2 contains a drafting error in the negation of its precondition. This assumption is held provisionally; it is not applied to the candidate without requester confirmation.\n\n4. **A4 (provisional):** \"Dashboard\" in R2 refers to a personalized view scoped to a specific authenticated user's data. If it is a generic, non-personalized view, the ownership reference \"their\" would be meaningless.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\n**R1** uses EARS event-driven surface syntax (`When`) but provides no usable trigger, no named system, and no observable response. The word \"appropriately\" is explicitly flagged as a vague qualifier in the readiness gate. The source statement is not a refinement of a prior requirement and carries no rationale explaining what domain or interaction it governs.\n\n**R2** uses a state-driven surface syntax (`While`) but its precondition and response clause are contradictory. The phrase \"no authenticated user exists\" scopes out the very actor (\"the user\") named in the response, making the requirement either vacuous (never satisfiable) or a drafting error with an inverted condition. The reference to \"their dashboard\" compounds the ambiguity: ownership cannot be resolved when the precondition excludes any authenticated user.\n\nBoth statements fail the readiness gate on implementability and verification grounds. Neither can be drafted as a normative EARS sentence without guessing material behavior.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Source | Status | Blocking issues |\n|-----------|--------|--------|-----------------|\n| Candidate 1 | R1 | **needs clarification** | No system named; \"complete\" undefined; \"appropriately\" has no observable measure; pattern unresolved |\n| Candidate 2 | R2 | **needs clarification** | No system named; precondition contradicts response actor; \"their dashboard\" undefined; pattern unresolved; vacuous / internally contradictory |\n\n**Neither candidate is ready for review.** Both require answers to the clarifying questions in the Clarifying Questions section before normative EARS sentences can be drafted. The highest-priority resolution items are:\n\n1. For R1: define the system, the trigger event, and what \"appropriately\" means in observable terms.\n2. For R2: resolve whether the precondition should be negated or inverted, name the system, and define \"dashboard\" content.\n"},"output":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: artifacts/result.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"artifacts/result.md\", \"content\": \"# Requirements Elicitation Package\\n\\n## Understanding\\n\\n**Source statements evaluated:**\\n\\n- **R1:** \\\"When a request is complete, it shall process it appropriately.\\\"\\n- **R2:** \\\"While no authenticated user exists, the user shall see their dashboard.\\\"\\n\\n**Requested outcome:** Review and evaluate two existing requirement statements as a requirements elicitor, producing a complete portable Requirements Elicitation Package.\\n\\n**Proposed system boundary:** Unknown for both statements. Neither names a system, an actor, an interface, or a domain. Both require significant clarification before they can be implemented or verified.\\n\\n**Identified information kinds before drafting:**\\n\\n| Statement | Kind | Notes |\\n|-----------|------|-------|\\n| R1 | Behavioral obligation (partial) | Names no system, no actor, no defined request lifecycle, no defined completion event, no defined response |\\n| R1 \\\"appropriately\\\" | Vague qualifier | Flagged per readiness gate; not a defined measure |\\n| R2 | Behavioral obligation (partial) | Names no system; precondition is internally contradictory (see Consistency Findings) |\\n| R2 \\\"no authenticated user exists\\\" | Precondition | Contradicts \\\"the user\\\" in the response clause |\\n| R2 \\\"their dashboard\\\" | Undefined domain term | Ownership reference unclear when no user is authenticated |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Clarifying Questions\\n\\n**R1 — high-value questions (any one of these blocks readiness):**\\n\\n1. **System boundary:** What system receives and processes the request? (e.g., an API gateway, a job scheduler, a payment processor?)\\n2. **Actor and interface:** Who or what submits the request, and at which boundary does the response appear?\\n3. **\\\"Complete\\\" defined:** What does it mean for a request to be complete — submitted, validated, fully executed, or acknowledged? Is \\\"complete\\\" an intermediate lifecycle state or a terminal one?\\n4. **\\\"Process it appropriately\\\" defined:** What observable action must the system take? (e.g., return a confirmation, write a record, forward to another system?) \\\"Appropriately\\\" has no agreed measure; two implementers would choose different behavior.\\n5. **Failure and alternate paths:** What shall the system do if the request cannot be processed, is malformed, arrives out of order, or arrives after a timeout?\\n\\n**R2 — high-value questions (any one of these blocks readiness):**\\n\\n1. **System boundary:** What system is responsible for showing the dashboard?\\n2. **Intended precondition:** The phrase \\\"while no authenticated user exists\\\" combined with \\\"the user shall see their dashboard\\\" is internally contradictory. Which behavior is intended:\\n   - (a) While no user is authenticated (i.e., prior to login), the system shall show a generic/public landing page?\\n   - (b) While an authenticated user exists (i.e., after login), the system shall show that user's personalized dashboard?\\n   - (c) Something else?\\n3. **\\\"Their dashboard\\\" defined:** Whose dashboard? If no authenticated user exists, there is no \\\"their.\\\" If an authenticated user does exist, what content constitutes \\\"their dashboard\\\"?\\n4. **Actor:** Who is \\\"the user\\\" — an unauthenticated visitor, an authenticated account holder, a specific role?\\n5. **Interface:** At what boundary is the dashboard visible — a browser viewport, an API response, a mobile screen?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Candidate Requirements\\n\\n### Candidate 1 (from R1)\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** unresolved\\n- **Requirement:** Drafting is blocked. The pattern cannot be selected without guessing. The source uses `When`, which would suggest an event-driven pattern, but \\\"a request is complete\\\" is not a defined discrete event at any named boundary. The response \\\"process it appropriately\\\" is not an observable system behavior. No system, actor, or interface is named.\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R1 appears to intend that some system shall perform some defined action after a request reaches a defined completion state. The intent cannot be preserved normatively without answers to the clarifying questions above.\\n\\n**Implementability contract**\\n- System boundary: **open** — not named in source\\n- Responsible system: **open** — not named in source\\n- Actor: **open** — not named in source\\n- Affected interfaces: **open** — not named in source\\n- Inputs and data definitions: **open** — \\\"a request\\\" is undefined; type, schema, and lifecycle state are unknown\\n- Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: **open** — \\\"complete\\\" is undefined; whether this is a state or a discrete event is unresolved\\n- Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: **open** — \\\"appropriately\\\" has no agreed measure; no alternate or failure response is stated\\n- Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **open** — none stated\\n- Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"request,\\\" \\\"complete,\\\" and \\\"appropriately\\\" are all undefined domain terms; pattern choice (event-driven vs. state-driven vs. complex) is unresolved\\n\\n**Verification contract**\\n- Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: **open** — no defined request type, no defined completion event, no named system to exercise\\n- Observable evidence or oracle: **open** — \\\"appropriately\\\" is not observable\\n- Expected result: **open** — unknown\\n- Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: **open** — none can be stated\\n- Boundary, negative, and failure cases: **open** — incomplete request, malformed request, duplicate request, concurrent completion events — all unaddressed\\n- Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: **open** — unknown\\n- Observable at the named boundary alone: no (no boundary is named)\\n- If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: cannot be determined until system boundary is defined\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Candidate 2 (from R2)\\n\\n- **Status:** needs clarification\\n- **Template:** unresolved\\n- **Requirement:** Drafting is blocked. The precondition \\\"while no authenticated user exists\\\" is vacuous or internally contradictory when combined with the response \\\"the user shall see their dashboard.\\\" If no authenticated user exists, there is no referent for \\\"the user\\\" or \\\"their dashboard.\\\" The EARS pattern cannot be confirmed as state-driven or event-driven without resolving the intended condition. Two interpretations produce opposite behavior:\\n\\n  - **Interpretation A (vacuous / unauthenticated state):** `While no authenticated session exists, the <system> shall display a <public/generic landing page> to the visitor.` — The dashboard reference is incorrect; this reads as a pre-login state.\\n  - **Interpretation B (likely intended — authenticated state):** `While an authenticated user session exists, the <system> shall display that user's dashboard.` — This inverts the precondition; \\\"no authenticated user\\\" becomes \\\"authenticated user.\\\"\\n\\n  Neither interpretation can be confirmed without the requester's input.\\n\\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R2 appears to intend that a named system show a personalized view to an authenticated user, but the precondition contradicts the response. The source may contain a drafting error (negation applied incorrectly).\\n\\n**Implementability contract**\\n- System boundary: **open** — not named in source\\n- Responsible system: **open** — not named in source\\n- Actor: **open** — \\\"the user\\\" is undefined when no authenticated user exists; contradictory referent\\n- Affected interfaces: **open** — not named in source\\n- Inputs and data definitions: **open** — \\\"dashboard\\\" content and \\\"authenticated user\\\" definition are undefined\\n- Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: **open** — \\\"no authenticated user exists\\\" contradicts \\\"the user\\\"; intended precondition is unresolved\\n- Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: **open** — \\\"see their dashboard\\\" is partially observable (a display appears) but content and scope are undefined; what is shown when no user is authenticated is unresolved\\n- Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **open** — none stated\\n- Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \\\"authenticated user,\\\" \\\"dashboard,\\\" and ownership reference \\\"their\\\" are undefined; the intended precondition is materially unresolved\\n\\n**Verification contract**\\n- Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: **open** — cannot be defined until the intended precondition is resolved; no named system to exercise\\n- Observable evidence or oracle: partially observable (a UI display or API response could be inspected) but content specification is unknown\\n- Expected result: **open** — unknown until precondition and dashboard definition are resolved\\n- Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: **open** — none can be stated\\n- Boundary, negative, and failure cases: **open** — what happens when a session expires mid-view? What if multiple authenticated users share a device? All unaddressed.\\n- Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: **open** — unknown\\n- Observable at the named boundary alone: no (no boundary is named)\\n- If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: cannot be determined until system boundary is defined\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\\n\\nThe following suggestions are grounded in the source statements. They are not approved requirements. Each should be accepted or rejected by the requester.\\n\\n**Relating to R1:**\\n\\n1. **(Required companion)** A requirement defining what constitutes a \\\"complete\\\" request is needed before R1 can be implemented or verified. Without a defined completion state or event, the trigger in R1 is meaningless.\\n\\n2. **(Failure-path companion)** A requirement defining what the system shall do if a request cannot be processed (malformed, unauthorized, resource unavailable) is implied by any processing obligation and should be stated.\\n\\n3. **(Boundary companion)** A requirement defining the maximum time allowed between request completion and the system's response is implied if latency is a concern.\\n\\n**Relating to R2:**\\n\\n4. **(Required companion)** A requirement defining what constitutes an authenticated user session — how it is established, how it expires, and what identifies it — is needed before R2 can be implemented or verified under either interpretation.\\n\\n5. **(Required companion)** A requirement defining the content and scope of \\\"the dashboard\\\" is needed. What data sources, elements, or views constitute the dashboard for a given user?\\n\\n6. **(Failure-path companion)** A requirement defining what the system shall display when a session expires while the user is viewing the dashboard is implied by a session-scoped obligation.\\n\\n7. **(Failure-path companion — Interpretation A only)** If R2 intends an unauthenticated state, a companion requirement defining what the system shall display (a login prompt, a public landing page, an error) is necessary.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Consistency Findings\\n\\n### Finding 1 — Vacuous precondition in R2 (Infeasible / vacuous)\\n\\n**Affected statement:** R2  \\n**Condition:** \\\"While no authenticated user exists\\\" combined with \\\"the user shall see their dashboard\\\"  \\n**Consequence:** The pronoun \\\"the user\\\" has no referent when the precondition holds. If no authenticated user exists, there is no \\\"their dashboard.\\\" The requirement cannot be satisfied: the precondition excludes the very actor the response requires. Under standard semantics, this statement is either vacuously true (the response is never triggered because the actor never exists) or a drafting error that inverts the intended condition.  \\n**Resolution options:**\\n- (a) Confirm that the precondition is inverted and correct it to \\\"While an authenticated user session exists\\\" — which would make this a state-driven requirement for authenticated users.\\n- (b) Confirm that the intent is an unauthenticated/pre-login state, and replace \\\"the user shall see their dashboard\\\" with a defined response for unauthenticated visitors (e.g., display a login page).\\n- (c) Ask the requester to restate the intended behavior.\\n\\n### Finding 2 — R1 and R2 relationship unknown (Gap / Precedence question)\\n\\n**Affected statements:** R1, R2  \\n**Condition:** R1 references \\\"a request\\\" being complete; R2 references viewing a dashboard. If the dashboard is loaded by processing a request, R1 may be intended to govern the same interaction as R2, or they may be entirely independent.  \\n**Consequence:** If they are related, a precedence or sequencing rule is missing. If they are independent, no conflict exists.  \\n**Resolution options:**\\n- Ask whether R1 and R2 describe the same user journey or different system behaviors.\\n\\n### Finding 3 — Vague qualifiers block both candidates (Gap)\\n\\n**Affected statements:** R1 (\\\"appropriately\\\"), R2 (\\\"their dashboard\\\")  \\n**Condition:** Both statements contain terms without agreed measures.  \\n**Consequence:** Two reasonable implementers would produce different observable behavior for each.  \\n**Resolution options:**\\n- Define \\\"appropriately\\\" as one or more named observable responses.\\n- Define \\\"their dashboard\\\" as a named set of content, data, or views scoped to the authenticated user's identity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n1. **A1 (provisional):** R1 and R2 are intended to describe system obligations, not user or actor obligations. The subject \\\"it\\\" in R1 and \\\"the user\\\" in R2 are interpreted as referring to distinct parties — a system and an actor respectively — but this is unconfirmed.\\n\\n2. **A2 (provisional):** \\\"Authenticated user\\\" in R2 refers to a user whose identity has been verified by a login or session mechanism at the system boundary, not merely a user who has visited the system.\\n\\n3. **A3 (provisional):** R2 contains a drafting error in the negation of its precondition. This assumption is held provisionally; it is not applied to the candidate without requester confirmation.\\n\\n4. **A4 (provisional):** \\\"Dashboard\\\" in R2 refers to a personalized view scoped to a specific authenticated user's data. If it is a generic, non-personalized view, the ownership reference \\\"their\\\" would be meaningless.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rationale and Context\\n\\n**R1** uses EARS event-driven surface syntax (`When`) but provides no usable trigger, no named system, and no observable response. The word \\\"appropriately\\\" is explicitly flagged as a vague qualifier in the readiness gate. The source statement is not a refinement of a prior requirement and carries no rationale explaining what domain or interaction it governs.\\n\\n**R2** uses a state-driven surface syntax (`While`) but its precondition and response clause are contradictory. The phrase \\\"no authenticated user exists\\\" scopes out the very actor (\\\"the user\\\") named in the response, making the requirement either vacuous (never satisfiable) or a drafting error with an inverted condition. The reference to \\\"their dashboard\\\" compounds the ambiguity: ownership cannot be resolved when the precondition excludes any authenticated user.\\n\\nBoth statements fail the readiness gate on implementability and verification grounds. Neither can be drafted as a normative EARS sentence without guessing material behavior.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Review Status\\n\\n| Candidate | Source | Status | Blocking issues |\\n|-----------|--------|--------|-----------------|\\n| Candidate 1 | R1 | **needs clarification** | No system named; \\\"complete\\\" undefined; \\\"appropriately\\\" has no observable measure; pattern unresolved |\\n| Candidate 2 | R2 | **needs clarification** | No system named; precondition contradicts response actor; \\\"their dashboard\\\" undefined; pattern unresolved; vacuous / internally contradictory |\\n\\n**Neither candidate is ready for review.** Both require answers to the clarifying questions in the Clarifying Questions section before normative EARS sentences can be drafted. The highest-priority resolution items are:\\n\\n1. For R1: define the system, the trigger event, and what \\\"appropriately\\\" means in observable terms.\\n2. For R2: resolve whether the precondition should be negated or inverted, name the system, and define \\\"dashboard\\\" content.\\n\"}"},"time":{"start":1791982634910,"end":1791982708654}}}}
